"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunkgrc||=[]).push([[31760],{99578(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"citeKey":"chen2012decoupledexecution","slug":"chen-2012-decoupled-execution-e1b0","title":"A Decoupled Execution Paradigm for Data-Intensive High-End Computing","authors":["Y. Chen","C. Chen","X.-H. Sun","W. D. Gropp","R. Thakur"],"venue":"IEEE International Conference on Cluster Computing 2012 (Cluster\'12), Beijing, China,","type":"Conference","entryType":"inproceedings","date":"September, 2012","year":2012,"era":"iit","month":9,"doi":"10.1109/cluster.2012.80","abstract":"High-end computing (HEC) applications in critical areas of science and technology tend to be more and more data intensive. I/O has become a vital performance bottleneck of modern HEC practice. Conventional HEC execution paradigms, however, are computing-centric for computation intensive applications. They are designed to utilize memory and CPU performance and have inherent limitations in addressing the critical I/O bottleneck issues of HEC. In this study, we propose a decoupled execution paradigm (DEP) to address the challenging I/O bottleneck issues. DEP is the first paradigm enabling users to identify and handle data- intensive operations separately. It can significantly reduce costly data movement and is better than the existing execution paradigms for data-intensive applications. The initial experimental tests have confirmed its promising potential. Its data-centric architecture could have an impact in future HEC systems, programming models, and algorithms design development. and","hasAbstract":true,"lede":"In this study, we propose a decoupled execution paradigm (DEP) to address the challenging I/O bottleneck issues.","links":{"pdf":"https://grc.iit.edu/papers/2012/chen-2012-decoupled-execution-e1b0.pdf"},"_projectIds":[],"grcSelected":false,"status":"published","visibility":"public","projectIds":["dep"],"memberAuthors":["sun-xian-he"]}')}}]);